GPU ChIMES NVE test
===================

Overview
--------
NVE ensemble run of 512 carbon atoms using the Carbon-2.0 (2+3+4B,
Tersoff cutoff) ChIMES force field, executed with the CUDA GPU path.

The test verifies that GPU-computed energies and forces produce the
same macroscopic thermodynamics as the CPU reference (stored in
../test_suite-NVE/expected_output/).  A tolerance of 1e-3 is used
instead of the CPU-to-CPU value of 1e-5 to accommodate expected
non-determinism from GPU floating-point atomicAdd reductions.

Files
-----
in.lammps              LAMMPS input (identical physics to test_suite-NVE,
                        references the same data/parameter files)
run_gpu_test.sh        Interactive runner (use inside an idev GPU session)
submit_stampede3.slurm SLURM batch script for Stampede3 GPU partitions
compare_gpu.py         Comparison script with configurable tolerance


Prerequisites
-------------
1. The GPU-enabled LAMMPS binary must be built first.

   First identify your GPU's SM architecture:
       nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,compute_cap --format=csv,noheader

   Then build (example for Blackwell SM 120 on Stampede3):
       cd <repo>/etc/lmp
       module load gcc/13.2.0 cuda/12.8
       ./install_gpu.sh 120     # replace 120 with your SM arch
       # Binary: etc/lmp/exe/lmp_mpi_chimes_gpu

   Common SM arch values:
       120  RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell / B200
        90  H100
        80  A100
        86  A30 / A40 / RTX 3090
        89  L40S / RTX 4090
        70  V100

2. A GPU compute node (login nodes do not have GPUs).


Running on Stampede3 -- interactive session
-------------------------------------------
# Step 1: obtain an interactive GPU node
idev -p rtx-small -N 1 -n 1 -t 00:30:00 -A <YOUR_ALLOCATION>
# (or use -p h100 for H100 nodes)

# Step 2: load modules matching the build
module load intel/24.0 impi/21.11 gcc/13.2.0 cuda/12.8 python/3.12.11

# Step 3: run the test
cd <repo>/etc/lmp/tests/test_suite-GPU_NVE
./run_gpu_test.sh

Expected output (current_output/):
  out.lammps   -- LAMMPS stdout (thermo table)
  log.lammps   -- LAMMPS log file
  compare.log  -- empty if PASS, discrepancies listed if FAIL


Running on Stampede3 -- batch submission
----------------------------------------
# The allocation is already set in submit_stampede3.slurm (TG-CHM250118).
# Edit the file if you need a different allocation or partition.

cd <repo>/etc/lmp/tests/test_suite-GPU_NVE
sbatch submit_stampede3.slurm

Monitor with:  squeue -u $USER
Results in:    current_output/


Manual one-liner (from an interactive GPU node)
-----------------------------------------------
cd <repo>/etc/lmp/tests/test_suite-GPU_NVE/current_output
cp ../in.lammps .
cp ../../test_suite-NVE/diamond_3.21gcc_512atoms.data.in .
cp ../../../serial_interface/tests/force_fields/published_params.Carbon-2.0.Small.2+3+4b.Tersoff.txt params.txt
srun -n 1 ../../exe/lmp_mpi_chimes_gpu -i in.lammps | tee out.lammps
python3 ../compare_gpu.py ../../test_suite-NVE/expected_output/log.lammps \
        log.lammps 1e-3


Tolerance rationale
-------------------
GPU floating-point sums are non-associative because multiple CUDA threads
atomically accumulate forces and energies using atomicAdd.  The order of
additions depends on thread scheduling and varies between runs.  For
double-precision IEEE 754 arithmetic this causes per-value differences on
the order of a few ULP (units in the last place), which propagates to
~1e-4 differences in per-step thermo quantities after 100 steps.  A
tolerance of 1e-3 provides comfortable margin while still catching genuine
physics errors.


Known issues / implementation notes
------------------------------------
* Per-thread stack: the k3B and k4B kernels require ~1.5 KB and ~3 KB of
  thread-local storage for Chebyshev arrays, exceeding the CUDA default of
  1 KB.  cudaDeviceSetLimit(cudaLimitStackSize, 8192) is called at
  initialization; no user action required.

* Batch pre-allocation: host batch arrays for 2B/3B/4B interactions are
  pre-sized to the full count before filling.  An earlier version grew the
  arrays on demand, dropping already-written entries on each reallocation and
  sending garbage atom-type indices to the GPU — the root cause of the
  initial cudaErrorIllegalAddress crash during development.
